<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Logging configuration.

  FOUR FILES, because four different people ask four different questions:

    app.log       "what happened around 14:32?"          everything, the running narrative
    business.log  "what did the system DO?"              imports, scheduler runs, sheet events
    audit.log     "who changed this row?"                one line per audited change
    error.log     "what broke, and what was the cause?"  errors only, root cause first

  Audit used to live in business.log at one line per changed FIELD. On the live database that
  was 42,498 audit lines against ~40 real business events — the file answering "what did the
  system do" was 99.8% something else. Splitting them is the single biggest readability win
  here, and it is why audit.log exists.

  TWO FORMATS, selected by the `json-logs` Spring profile:

    (default)          text — human-first. What someone reads over SSH in production.
    SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=json-logs
                       one JSON object per line, for Filebeat → Logstash/Elasticsearch.

  Both write the same four files with the same names, so nothing downstream has to care which
  one is active. Ship-to-ELK becomes one environment variable rather than a config rewrite —
  and, importantly, avoids asking Logstash to grok a layout designed for human eyes, which is
  where multi-line stack traces and Persian message text cause endless trouble.

  WHY A SPRING PROFILE rather than a plain property. Logback's own <if condition="…"> needs
  Janino, its `condition` attribute is deprecated in logback 1.5, and nesting <if> inside an
  <appender> raises IfNestedWithinSecondPhaseElementSC. <springProfile> is Spring Boot's own
  extension and needs no extra dependency. Note it composes: with another profile already in
  play, use SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod,json-logs.

  THE ELEVEN WARNINGS AT STARTUP ARE FROM THIS FILE, AND THEY ARE EXPECTED:

    |-WARN … SpringProfileIfNestedWithinSecondPhaseElementSanityChecker
       <springProfile> elements cannot be nested within an <appender>, <logger> or <root>

  Spring Boot documents <springProfile> as a top-level element, and each of the nine appenders
  below nests two of them to choose its encoder. **The selection nevertheless works** — verified,
  not assumed: under `json-logs` every appender resolves to LogstashEncoder, and without it to
  PatternLayoutEncoder. `JsonLogProfileIntegrationTest` and `PlainTextLogProfileIntegrationTest`
  assert exactly that, in both directions, so a future Spring Boot release that really does stop
  honouring the nesting fails the build instead of quietly shipping human-readable text to a
  Logstash pipeline that cannot parse it. That is the risk worth guarding; the console noise is
  not.

  Removing the warnings means lifting <springProfile> to the top level, which means declaring all
  nine appenders twice — rolling policies, paths and size caps included — in a file that governs
  production log rotation. That trade was judged the wrong way round: doubling this file to
  silence a startup message is a far better chance of losing logs than the message is.

  TIMESTAMPS are ISO-8601 in Asia/Tehran WITH the offset (…+03:30). Local time so an operator
  reads the same clock as the wall; explicit offset so the value is still unambiguous after it
  reaches a log store, and can be lined up with the database's epoch-millis columns.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-->
<configuration>

    <springProperty scope="context" name="LOG_PATH" source="app.log.path" defaultValue="ProdLog"/>
    <springProperty scope="context" name="LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE" source="app.log.max-file-size" defaultValue="100MB"/>

    <property name="TZ" value="Asia/Tehran"/>
    <property name="TS" value="%d{yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSXXX, ${TZ}}"/>

    <!--
      The general line. Field order follows how a person actually scans:
        WHEN   level  WHICH THREAD  WHICH REQUEST  WHO@WHERE-FROM  WHAT THEY ASKED FOR  source

      %thread is NOT redundant with the correlation id, which is the tempting thing to assume.
      AsyncConfig.wrapWithMdc copies the whole MDC onto async workers, so a line written by the
      import pool minutes after the upload still carries that request's correlationId, user and
      even its URI:

        [import-1] [3e8f33d6-b2b] admin POST /batch-import … [IMPORT_DONE] rowsRead=3000

      Without the thread there is nothing to distinguish that from a line on the HTTP thread.
      Scheduler lines have no correlation id at all ([-]), so there the thread is the only
      handle. And this codebase has real concurrency to debug — see the several *RaceIntegration
      tests.

      Deliberately absent:
        layer     — LoggingAspect already prints ">>> [WEB]" inside the message itself.
        failedAt  — empty on all but error lines; it is in the error.log pattern instead.
    -->
    <property name="APP_PATTERN"
              value="${TS} %-5level [%thread] [%X{correlationId:--}] %X{user:-anonymous}@%X{clientIp:--} %X{method:-} %X{uri:-} %logger{40} - %msg%n"/>

    <!--
      error.log gets its own, deliberately louder layout.

      A blank line and a rule before each entry: the previous file had 16 message lines against
      904 stack-frame lines, so the thing you are looking for was invisible between walls of
      "at org.springframework…". The separator makes each error one visually obvious block, and
      answers the question "where does this stack trace end and the next error begin".

      errorId is repeated in the header for scanning, and LoggingAspect also puts it in the
      message text so it survives into app.log's propagation lines — that is what ties the two
      files together, and what a person quotes in a support message. Two requests failing in
      the same second cannot be told apart by timestamp alone.

      %rEx — ROOT CAUSE FIRST. The actual problem is on the first line instead of forty frames
      of proxy and filter wrapping below it. Depth-capped for the same reason.
    -->
    <!-- Note the two spaces after every %n: logback's parser reads "%n" followed immediately by
         letters as one conversion word ("%nfailedAt"), which fails config validation outright.
         The indent they produce is wanted anyway — it marks the continuation lines of an entry. -->
    <property name="ERROR_PATTERN"
              value="%n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────%n${TS} %-5level errorId=%X{errorId:--} [%X{correlationId:--}] %X{user:-anonymous}@%X{clientIp:--} %X{method:-} %X{uri:-} thread=%thread%n  failedAt=%X{failedAt:--}%n  %logger{60} - %msg%n%rEx{30}"/>

    <!-- Thread matters here more than anywhere: these events come from the import pool and the
         scheduler as often as from a request, and that is exactly what you want to know first. -->
    <property name="BUSINESS_PATTERN"
              value="${TS} %-5level [%thread] [%X{correlationId:--}] %X{user:-system} %msg%n"/>

    <!-- No correlation id or URI: an audit row is about an entity and an actor, and the actor
         is already a named column in the message. The thread stays because it separates a change
         somebody made from one the scheduler made (auto-expiry raises status requests). -->
    <property name="AUDIT_PATTERN"
              value="${TS} [%thread] %msg%n"/>

    <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ CONSOLE -->
    <appender name="CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <springProfile name="json-logs">
                <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
                    <timeZone>${TZ}</timeZone>
                    <timestampPattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSXXX</timestampPattern>
                    <!-- The logback context carries LOG_PATH and LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE; without this they are
                         repeated as fields on every single event. -->
                    <includeContext>false</includeContext>

                    <!-- Internal bookkeeping for "has this exception already been logged"; errorId is the

                         field that means anything to a reader. -->

                    <excludeMdcKeyName>loggedThrowableId</excludeMdcKeyName>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
        <springProfile name="!json-logs">
                <encoder>
                    <pattern>${APP_PATTERN}</pattern>
                    <charset>UTF-8</charset>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
    </appender>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ APP (all layers) -->
    <appender name="FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender">
        <file>${LOG_PATH}/app.log</file>
        <!--
          Size AND time. Time alone caps only the archive: the *current* day's file could grow
          without limit, and one bad afternoon of DEBUG on a bulk import can fill the disk the
          application is also writing attachments to.
        -->
        <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy">
            <fileNamePattern>${LOG_PATH}/ArchiveLog/app-%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.%i.log.gz</fileNamePattern>
            <maxFileSize>${LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE}</maxFileSize>
            <maxHistory>90</maxHistory>
            <totalSizeCap>2GB</totalSizeCap>
        </rollingPolicy>
        <springProfile name="json-logs">
                <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
                    <timeZone>${TZ}</timeZone>
                    <timestampPattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSXXX</timestampPattern>
                    <!-- The logback context carries LOG_PATH and LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE; without this they are
                         repeated as fields on every single event. -->
                    <includeContext>false</includeContext>

                    <!-- Internal bookkeeping for "has this exception already been logged"; errorId is the

                         field that means anything to a reader. -->

                    <excludeMdcKeyName>loggedThrowableId</excludeMdcKeyName>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
        <springProfile name="!json-logs">
                <encoder>
                    <pattern>${APP_PATTERN}</pattern>
                    <charset>UTF-8</charset>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
    </appender>

    <appender name="ASYNC_FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.classic.AsyncAppender">
        <queueSize>2048</queueSize>
        <discardingThreshold>0</discardingThreshold>
        <appender-ref ref="FILE"/>
    </appender>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ BUSINESS events only -->
    <appender name="BUSINESS_FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender">
        <file>${LOG_PATH}/business.log</file>
        <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy">
            <fileNamePattern>${LOG_PATH}/ArchiveLog/business-%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.%i.log.gz</fileNamePattern>
            <maxFileSize>${LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE}</maxFileSize>
            <maxHistory>90</maxHistory>
            <totalSizeCap>1GB</totalSizeCap>
        </rollingPolicy>
        <springProfile name="json-logs">
                <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
                    <timeZone>${TZ}</timeZone>
                    <timestampPattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSXXX</timestampPattern>
                    <!-- The logback context carries LOG_PATH and LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE; without this they are
                         repeated as fields on every single event. -->
                    <includeContext>false</includeContext>

                    <!-- Internal bookkeeping for "has this exception already been logged"; errorId is the

                         field that means anything to a reader. -->

                    <excludeMdcKeyName>loggedThrowableId</excludeMdcKeyName>
                    <customFields>{"stream":"business"}</customFields>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
        <springProfile name="!json-logs">
                <encoder>
                    <pattern>${BUSINESS_PATTERN}</pattern>
                    <charset>UTF-8</charset>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
    </appender>

    <appender name="ASYNC_BUSINESS" class="ch.qos.logback.classic.AsyncAppender">
        <queueSize>512</queueSize>
        <discardingThreshold>0</discardingThreshold>
        <appender-ref ref="BUSINESS_FILE"/>
    </appender>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ AUDIT trail only -->
    <appender name="AUDIT_FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender">
        <file>${LOG_PATH}/audit.log</file>
        <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy">
            <fileNamePattern>${LOG_PATH}/ArchiveLog/audit-%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.%i.log.gz</fileNamePattern>
            <maxFileSize>${LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE}</maxFileSize>
            <!-- Longer than app/business: this is the "who did it" record, and the same
                 reasoning that gives error.log 180 days applies here. The authoritative copy
                 is the audit_log table, which has its own retention setting. -->
            <maxHistory>180</maxHistory>
            <totalSizeCap>2GB</totalSizeCap>
        </rollingPolicy>
        <springProfile name="json-logs">
                <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
                    <timeZone>${TZ}</timeZone>
                    <timestampPattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSXXX</timestampPattern>
                    <!-- The logback context carries LOG_PATH and LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE; without this they are
                         repeated as fields on every single event. -->
                    <includeContext>false</includeContext>

                    <!-- Internal bookkeeping for "has this exception already been logged"; errorId is the

                         field that means anything to a reader. -->

                    <excludeMdcKeyName>loggedThrowableId</excludeMdcKeyName>
                    <customFields>{"stream":"audit"}</customFields>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
        <springProfile name="!json-logs">
                <encoder>
                    <pattern>${AUDIT_PATTERN}</pattern>
                    <charset>UTF-8</charset>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
    </appender>

    <!--
      Audit is the one high-volume stream that may be dropped rather than block a request.
      queueSize is large and discardingThreshold stays 0 (never discard) because losing an
      audit line silently would be worse than a slow request — but a bulk import can enqueue
      tens of thousands of these, which is why the queue is sized for it.
    -->
    <appender name="ASYNC_AUDIT" class="ch.qos.logback.classic.AsyncAppender">
        <queueSize>8192</queueSize>
        <discardingThreshold>0</discardingThreshold>
        <appender-ref ref="AUDIT_FILE"/>
    </appender>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ ERRORS only -->
    <appender name="ERROR_FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender">
        <file>${LOG_PATH}/error.log</file>
        <filter class="ch.qos.logback.classic.filter.ThresholdFilter">
            <level>ERROR</level>
        </filter>
        <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy">
            <fileNamePattern>${LOG_PATH}/ArchiveLog/error-%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.%i.log.gz</fileNamePattern>
            <maxFileSize>${LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE}</maxFileSize>
            <maxHistory>180</maxHistory>
            <totalSizeCap>1GB</totalSizeCap>
        </rollingPolicy>
        <springProfile name="json-logs">
                <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
                    <timeZone>${TZ}</timeZone>
                    <timestampPattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSXXX</timestampPattern>
                    <!-- The logback context carries LOG_PATH and LOG_MAX_FILE_SIZE; without this they are
                         repeated as fields on every single event. -->
                    <includeContext>false</includeContext>

                    <!-- Internal bookkeeping for "has this exception already been logged"; errorId is the

                         field that means anything to a reader. -->

                    <excludeMdcKeyName>loggedThrowableId</excludeMdcKeyName>
                    <customFields>{"stream":"error"}</customFields>
                    <!-- Root cause first and depth-capped, matching the text layout: the same
                         entry should read the same way whichever format is active. -->
                    <throwableConverter class="net.logstash.logback.stacktrace.ShortenedThrowableConverter">
                        <rootCauseFirst>true</rootCauseFirst>
                        <maxDepthPerThrowable>30</maxDepthPerThrowable>
                        <maxLength>8192</maxLength>
                    </throwableConverter>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
        <springProfile name="!json-logs">
                <encoder>
                    <pattern>${ERROR_PATTERN}</pattern>
                    <charset>UTF-8</charset>
                </encoder>
        </springProfile>
    </appender>

    <appender name="ASYNC_ERROR" class="ch.qos.logback.classic.AsyncAppender">
        <queueSize>512</queueSize>
        <discardingThreshold>0</discardingThreshold>
        <appender-ref ref="ERROR_FILE"/>
    </appender>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ LOGGERS -->

    <!-- Layer trace: loggers are the target class (web/service/repository), not LoggingAspect -->
    <!-- WEB/API aspect entry-exit = INFO; SVC/REPO aspect entry-exit = DEBUG (bulk/import-safe).
         Explicit log.info and BusinessEventLogger remain visible at INFO. -->
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.web" level="INFO"/>
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.controller" level="INFO"/>
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.service" level="INFO"/>
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.repository" level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.aspect" level="INFO"/>
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.logging" level="INFO"/>

    <!-- Business/operational events → business.log. Synthetic logger name (no such package). -->
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.business" level="INFO" additivity="false">
        <appender-ref ref="ASYNC_BUSINESS"/>
        <appender-ref ref="ASYNC_ERROR"/>
    </logger>

    <!--
      Audit trail → audit.log ONLY. Synthetic name, matching …business; note it is deliberately
      not the real `…audit` package, which would also capture AuditEntitySupport's own logging.
      No ASYNC_ERROR ref: an audit row is never an error, and no console/app.log ref either —
      routing tens of thousands of lines a day back into app.log is the problem being fixed.
    -->
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst.audittrail" level="INFO" additivity="false">
        <appender-ref ref="ASYNC_AUDIT"/>
    </logger>

    <!-- WEB/SVC package loggers at INFO so intentional summaries stay; REPO noise off -->
    <logger name="com.hnp.backendofflinefirst" level="INFO" additivity="false">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
        <appender-ref ref="ASYNC_FILE"/>
        <appender-ref ref="ASYNC_ERROR"/>
    </logger>

    <!-- Quiet SQL chatter during Import/bulk (enable DEBUG only when diagnosing) -->
    <logger name="org.hibernate.SQL" level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind" level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="org.springframework.web" level="INFO"/>

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
        <appender-ref ref="ASYNC_FILE"/>
        <appender-ref ref="ASYNC_ERROR"/>
    </root>

</configuration>
